<section class="section section--tight" aria-labelledby="yield-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="yield-title">Yield is the number to watch</h2>
      <p>If 1,000 kg goes in and less comes out, the difference should be explained, not assumed. This is an illustration with invented figures, to show the calculation only.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="data-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Illustration of a yield calculation, scrolls sideways on small screens">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Item</th><th scope="col">Weight</th><th scope="col">Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Material issued</th><td>1,000 kg</td><td>Should match the stock register and the supplier's bill</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Good output</th><td>940 kg</td><td>Becomes finished stock, or goes back to the customer in job work</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Scrap recovered</th><td>45 kg</td><td>Should appear later as a scrap sale</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Unexplained loss</th><td>15 kg</td><td>Compare it with your normal rate. A sudden rise needs an explanation</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
    <p class="para">Record this by batch, grade or coil where you can. Grades of steel differ widely in price, so a loss in one grade is not the same as a loss in another.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="scrap-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="scrap-title">Selling scrap</h2>
      <p>Offcuts and rejected metal are real income for a unit like yours, and may also bring a duty to collect tax from the buyer. That comes from the table in section 394(1) of the Income-tax Act, 2025, which printed 1% for scrap when enacted. Since then, a June 2026 Railway Board letter has charged 2% and cited the Finance Act, 2026, so confirm today's figure before you rely on either.</p>
      <p class="para">Also check that your material meets the section's meaning of scrap, since usable metal may not. Some buyers can give a declaration that changes the position, and that form belongs in your file. Bill every scrap lot, even small ones. If the bills add up to far less than your yield record suggests, someone will ask why.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Working for others</h2>
      <p>If you process a customer's material for a charge, it is not your stock, and only the charge is your sale. Keep a register showing whose metal you hold, by weight.</p>
      <p class="para">If you are the customer, section 143 of the CGST Act keeps responsibility for accounts with you as principal. Inputs must be back, or supplied from there, within one year. See {% routeLink "/industries/accounting-for-manufacturers/", "accounting for manufacturers" %}. Also agree who keeps the scrap from the job, since this affects both parties' figures.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="closure-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="closure-title">If a process has stopped or moved</h2>
      <p>A Delhi Pollution Control Committee status report from 2020 records closure of steel pickling units in Wazirpur following National Green Tribunal directions, and removal of plant and machinery by some. We have not found a later position, so take current advice on what is allowed. This page does not suggest anything about any particular business.</p>
      <p class="para">If your unit changed its work, these points deserve a clear record:</p>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Machinery sold, scrapped or moved, with dates and values</li>
        <li>Closure, shifting and environmental compliance costs</li>
        <li>Work now sent to outside units, and any new place of business to add to your registration</li>
        <li>Stock and credit balances relating to the stopped process</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>What to ask an accountant</h2>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Have you worked with metal units, and how do you test yield?</li>
        <li>How do you value stock when prices move sharply?</li>
        <li>How will my job work register tie in with my GST filings?</li>
        <li>How do you treat machinery that is idle or has been sold?</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="para">AccountOrbit has no office in Wazirpur, and takes enquiries by phone from anywhere in Delhi. This page describes the area's trade, not an office.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

